data.content:
Here are the three EF Core 10 breaking changes affecting your code and how to fix them:

---

## 1. `ExecuteUpdateAsync` — now accepts a regular lambda

The API changed from `Expression<Func<...>>` to `Action<...>`. Your expression tree construction no longer compiles. Replace with a simple lambda:

```csharp
// .NET 10 — no expression trees needed
await context.Blogs.ExecuteUpdateAsync(s =>
{
    s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, 8);
    if (nameChanged)
    {
        s.SetProperty(b => b.Name, "foo");
    }
});
```

---

## 2. Complex type column names — now uniquified/fully-qualified

Two changes hit you:
- **Duplicate property names** (e.g., `ShippingAddress.Street` and `BillingAddress.Street`) now get a numeric suffix.
- **Nested complex types** use the full path: `Order.Payment.BillingAddress.City` → column `Payment_BillingAddress_City` (was `BillingAddress_City`).

A migration will be generated to rename columns. To **preserve existing column names**, pin them explicitly:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, sa =>
        sa.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("ShippingAddress_Street"));
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, ba =>
        ba.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("BillingAddress_Street"));
});

// Nested example
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment, p =>
        p.ComplexProperty(pay => pay.BillingAddress, ba =>
            ba.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("BillingAddress_City")));
});
```

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `logCommandText` parameter

All methods gained a `string logCommandText` parameter (redacted SQL for safe logging):

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        string logCommandText)   // ← add this
    {
        // Use logCommandText for logging (constants may be redacted)
        // Use command.CommandText for actual SQL
    }
}
```

---

**Summary:** The biggest win is #1 — you can delete all that expression-tree plumbing and use plain C# control flow inside the lambda. For #2, generate a migration and either accept the renames or pin with `HasColumnName`. For #3, just add the new parameter to your implementation.
